  "name": "Question 20",
  "problem": "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(\\pi t)-2t,\\,y={e}^{2t},\\,0<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nDetermine the value of the parameter $t$ at which the normal line to the curve is horizontal.",
  "worked_solution": "The normal line is horizontal when the tangent line to the curve is vertical. Therefore, we require ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${y}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{\u2032}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}-2,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=2{e}^{2t}\n$$\n\nSolving ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{t}-2=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n{y}^{\u2032}(\\frac{1}{2})=2{e}^{2\\times (1/2)}=2e\\ne 0,\\,\\,\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed horizontal when $t=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
